From 8052c56c81124ca203129e98b73aab3cf8d8339e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: shorinkiwata <fcl709@outlook.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 17:19:50 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 5/5] ntdll: Report written write-copy pages as read-write.

Windows tracks whether a write-copy page has been privately copied. Once
it has been written, VirtualQuery() and the old protection returned by
VirtualProtect() report PAGE_READWRITE (or PAGE_EXECUTE_READWRITE), and
the page stays copied across later protection changes. Pages that were
never written keep reporting PAGE_WRITECOPY.

Wine maps write-copy pages MAP_PRIVATE and writable (or reads unaligned
sections into anonymous memory), so the copy happens without Wine
noticing, and it always reported PAGE_WRITECOPY. Chromium relies on the
Windows behaviour: it makes a written global in a DLL's data section
read-only and CHECKs that VirtualProtect() returns PAGE_READWRITE as the
old protection, so CEF-based renderer processes crash right after start.

When the kernel supports asynchronous userfaultfd write protection (the
mechanism already used for write watches), register each image view once
it is mapped and relocated, and before reporting protections use
PAGEMAP_SCAN to set VPROT_COPIED on the write-copy pages written since.
Without kernel support nothing changes.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
